.he "ioctl (2)                    10/28/26                     ioctl (2)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ioctl
- control a device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
.bd
ioctl(fd, cmd, arg)
.br
.bd
int fd, cmd;
.br
.bd
char *arg;
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Ioctl
runs the request
.bd
cmd
against the driver of the device named by
.bd
fd.
A device is a block device or a character device, and the request
means a different thing on each.
.sp
On a block device
.bd
arg
is a
.bd
struct cdb
and the only request is
.bd
CDBCMD,
which hands the command block it describes to the driver
and lets it run.
The block is the bus's own - a SCSI command descriptor
block, the seven task-file registers of an ATA command,
or the HD-DMA board's block - and the kernel never looks
inside it.  A driver with no command block answers ENOTTY.

.bd
arg
points at a
.bd
struct cdb
(see <sys/ioctl.h>), and the structure's
.bd
cmd
holds up to sixteen bytes of command, while
.bd
len
says how many of those bytes are the command.
.bd
count
is the number of bytes in the data phase, or zero if
there is none.
.bd
flags
says which way the data goes: CDB_IN means the device
fills the buffer, and without it the buffer is sent.
CDB_ASYNC, for the network card only, says the caller
will read the answer from the card's frame stream
rather than have the call wait for it.
.bd
buf
is the caller's buffer for the data, and
.bd
hole
is where in
.bd
cmd
the address of that buffer is to be written, for a bus
whose command block carries one, or -1 for a bus that
does not.

A data phase of one disk block (512 bytes) or less is
moved by the kernel, and the caller's buffer is filled
from it or sent from it.  A larger one, up to CDBDATA
(one page), is moved by the driver itself.  The command
block is copied back to the caller when the driver
returns, including anything the driver wrote into it,
so a bus that answers a command inside its block leaves
its answer there.
.sp
On a character device
.bd
cmd
is one of the device's own request codes and
.bd
arg
is whatever that request wants.  A terminal has two:
.bd
TIOCGETA
writes the six-byte
.bd
struct sgtty
(see <sys/sgtty.h>) that
.bd
arg
points at, and
.bd
TIOCSETA
sets the terminal from it.  They are the requests behind
.bd
gtty
(3) and
.bd
stty
(3); those are the calls to make, and this is the call
under them.  Both were system calls once, and a binary
built before the library moved them here still reaches
the same two requests through the old numbers
(gtty = 32, stty = 31).  Any other character device has
its own requests - the network card's are the NET*
requests of <sys/net.h> - and a request its driver does
not have answers ENOTTY.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
0 if the request ran, or -1 if it did not.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ERRORS
.in +5
.br
.bd
EINVAL
if cmd is not CDBCMD, or len is not between 1 and
CDBMAX, or count is negative or greater than CDBDATA.
.br
.bd
EBADF
if fd is not an open file.
.br
.bd
ENOTTY
if fd is not a block or character device, or the device
does not have the request: a block driver with no command
block to run, or a character driver with no such request.
.br
.bd
EIO
if the command failed and the driver gave no other
reason.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ASSEMBLER
.in +5
.br
.bd
(ioctl = 54)
.br
.bd
(fd in hl, cmd and arg on the stack)
.br
.bd
sys; ioctl; cmd; arg
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
0 is returned on success.  The carry flag is set on
failure, and the error number is then in hl.
